make the helper return the lookup and call run once per test, with separate tests for the two case_sensitive assertions. Or keep two runs \u2014 it's fine. Hmm, but for clarity, let me do:\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_option_matching(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    content = \"[global]\\nKey = value\\n\"\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content=content, case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['Key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n\n\ndef test_case_sensitive_rejects_wrong_case(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    content = \"[global]\\nKey = value\\n\"\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content=content, case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\nCleaner. The second also covers var-None branch.\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 with case_sensitive=True, optionxform = to_native. Content 'Key = value' \u2192 option stored as 'Key'. Lookup 'key' \u2192 'key' in {'Key': 'value'}? No \u2192 default None \u2192 []. Good.\n\nAnd a case-insensitive control test:\n\n```python\ndef test_case_insensitive_by_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    content = \"[global]\\nKey = value\\n\"\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content=content)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\nWith case_sensitive=False, optionxform lowercases 'Key' \u2192 'key'; lookup 'key' \u2192 found \u2192 'value'. Good \u2014 verifies line 142 branch False behavior.\n\nNow \u2014 the helper writes the ini file to tmp_path. But since find_file_in_search_path and _get_file_contents are mocked, the file isn't strictly needed. Should I keep writing it? It makes the test more realistic and would catch mocking mistakes... but actually it's dead weight. Hmm. Let me keep it \u2014 harmless, and if someone removes the find_file mock, the test still... no, _get_file_contents is mocked too. Whatever, keep it for realism; it also documents the fixture. Actually, writing the file with the right encoding is nice. Keep