lue(self, key, section, dvalue, is_regexp)`. run calls `self.get_value(key, paramvals['section'], paramvals['default'], paramvals['re'])`. OK.\n\nNow let me think about whether `readfp` exists \u2014 `configparser.ConfigParser.readfp` is deprecated since 3.2 but still present (alias for read_file). In Python 3.12, `readfp` still exists (deprecated, emits DeprecationWarning). In Python 3.13? Hmm, `readfp` was slated for removal... As of Python 3.13, `readfp` still exists I believe (it's been deprecated forever but not removed). Actually, checking: Python 3.12 docs say \"Deprecated since version 3.2, removed in 3.12\"? No... Let me think. `RawConfigParser.readfp` \u2014 deprecated since 3.2. In CPython main, `readfp` still exists with a deprecation warning. I believe it was NOT removed in 3.12/3.13. Since ansible's own code uses it and ansible supports Python 3.9-3.13, it must exist. OK fine.\n\nNow \u2014 the DuplicateOptionError test: content '[global]\\nkey = a\\nkey = b\\n'. ConfigParser strict=True default \u2192 DuplicateOptionError raised during readfp. But wait \u2014 is strict default True? `ConfigParser(defaults=None, dict_type=dict, allow_no_value=False, *, comment_prefixes=..., inline_comment_prefixes=..., strict=True, ...)`. Yes, strict=True by default in Python 3. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 DuplicateOptionError: raised when strict=True and an option is duplicated within the same section. Yes.\n\nNow the missing-section test: I need to confirm get_value's behavior. If get_value catches NoSectionError \u2192 raises AnsibleLookupError(\"Can't find section nope\") \u2014 then run's except NoSectionError never triggers, and my `match=\"No section\"` assertion would FAIL. So I must check get_value's source via get_info before finalizing the match pattern. If get_value catches it, I'll assert `pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"Can't find section\")` and note that lines 196-197 are unreachable... but the task insists they're missing lines to cover