me also think about whether `pytest.raises(..., match=...)` patterns need re escaping: \"No section 'nope'\" \u2014 quotes fine. \"Could not use\" fine. \"Duplicate option\" fine. \"bogus is not a valid option\" fine. \"No key to lookup\" fine.\n\nOne more: for the ValueError test, the message includes `params` repr: \"from '['key=a=b']'\". Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 the `test_missing_section_raises`: section='nope'. run: file parsed OK ('[global]...'), then get_value('key','nope',None,False) \u2192 cp.get('nope','key') \u2192 NoSectionError \u2192 propagates (get_value doesn't catch NoSectionError \u2014 confirmed by get_info: only NoOptionError is caught) \u2192 run's except \u2192 AnsibleLookupError(\"No section 'nope' in ansible.ini\"). \n\nWait \u2014 double-check cp.get: `self.cp.get(section, key)` \u2014 ConfigParser.get(section, option) raises NoSectionError if section missing (no fallback passed). Yes. And get_value's try only catches NoOptionError. NoSectionError propagates. Confirmed.\n\nNow \u2014 hmm, one more consideration about `cp.get` and NoOptionError vs NoSectionError ordering: `_unify_values(section, vars)` raises NoSectionError first. Good.\n\nNow, let me also add a test for `re=True` + missing section? Not needed.\n\nWhat about `test_regexp_no_match`? get_value returns [] \u2192 var [] \u2192 not None \u2192 MutableSequence \u2192 loop empty \u2192 ret []. Covers 198-True/199-True with empty list. Optional \u2014 skip, we have enough.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. Let me reconsider one thing: `if var is not None:` \u2014 for the regexp-no-match case, var=[] \u2192 passes the not-None check \u2192 isinstance list \u2192 loop over [] \u2192 nothing. ret=[]. Not a distinct branch beyond what we have. Skip.\n\nNow \u2014 the `test_multiple_terms_accumulate`: terms ['key','other'] \u2192 first iteration: key='key' \u2192 'value'; second: key='other' \u2192 'ovalue'. ret ['value','ovalue']. Covers loop-back edge. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more potential gotcha: `paramvals` dict is shared between the two iterations (same dict). No inline params \u2192 no mutation. Good.\n\nNow, about cleaning: each test creates its own lookup and tmp_path (function-scoped fixtures) \u2192 no pollution. monkeypatch undoes instance attrs. \n\nLet me also double-check `test_case_insensitive_by_default`: content '[global]\\nKey = value\\n', case_sensitive=False \u2192 optionxform default (str.lower) \u2192 stored 'key'. term 'key' \u2192 cp.get('global','key') \u2192 optionxform('key')='key' \u2192 'value'. Good.\n\n`test_case_sensitive_match`: case_sensitive=True \u2192 optionxform=to_native \u2192 stored 'Key'. term 'Key' \u2192 cp.get \u2192 optionxform('Key') = to_native('Key') = 'Key' \u2192 found \u2192 'value'. Good.\n\n`test_case_sensitive_no_match`: term 'key' \u2192 optionxform('key')='key' \u2192 not in {'Key'} \u2192 NoOptionError \u2192 dflt None \u2192 var None \u2192 ret []. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 `to_native` as optionxform: `to_native('Key')` \u2192 'Key' (str passthrough on py3). Good.\n\nNow, let me write the final test file. Also, should I include a test where `allow_no_value` is explicitly False and file has no-value line \u2192 ParsingError propagates? That's an uncaught exception path \u2014 not in the missing lines. Skip.\n\nOne more thought \u2014 the task says \"lines 138-204 do not execute\" \u2014 so ALL of run() is uncovered, including get_value? No \u2014 get_value is defined before run (lines ~120-136), presumably covered or not required. The task only asks for 138-204. My tests focus there. get_value gets exercised as a side effect (necessary for run to work). Fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I worry that `LookupModule` in the installed ansible version differs from the snippet? The get_info output matches the snippet's structure (get_value with dflt param, _parse_params with defaultdict). The get_info get_value differs from what I remembered \u2014 good thing I checked! The get_info version:\n\n```python\ndef get_value(self, key, section, dflt, is_regexp):\n    if is_regexp:\n        return [v for k, v in self.cp.items(section) if re.match(key, k)]\n    value = None\n    try:\n        value = self.cp.get(section, key)\n    except configparser.NoOptionError:\n        return dflt\n    return value\n```\n\nThis matches ansible-core 2.12+ (after the NoSectionError refactor). And run() matches the snippet. Good.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one thing: in run(), `var = self.get_value(key, paramvals['section'], paramvals['default'], paramvals['re'])`. get_value's regexp branch returns a list \u2014 so `isinstance(var, MutableSequence)` \u2192 True \u2192 extend. And non-regexp returns str or dflt. Consistent with my tests.\n\nNow \u2014 the `default` option: in real DOCUMENTATION, default is a string. My list-default test is a bit artificial but valid for coverage. Keep it, with a comment.\n\nLet me also double check `_parse_params` edge: term 'key bogus=1' \u2014 I decided to use 'bogus=1' instead for a cleaner name. Trace 'bogus=1': idp=0, '=' in phrase \u2192 loop valid_keys: does any '%s=' % k appear in 'bogus=1'? 'file=' no, 'section=' no, 'default=' no, 're=' no, 'type=' no, 'encoding=' no, 'case_sensitive=' no, 'allow_no_value=' no. thiskey='key'. params['key']='bogus=1'. Return ['bogus=1']. run: split('=') \u2192 ['bogus','1'] \u2192 'bogus' not in paramvals \u2192 AnsibleLookupError('bogus is not a valid option.'). \n\nHmm wait \u2014 careful: 're=' \u2014 is 're=' a substring of 'bogus=1'? b-o-g-u-s-=-1 \u2192 no 're='. Good.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 what about 'type=' vs phrase 'subtype=x'? Not used. OK.\n\nNow, also \u2014 the inline test 'key file=ansible.ini section=global': note 'file=' check happens for k='file' \u2014 but ALSO check: does 're=' appear in 'file=ansible.ini'? No. Does 'type=' appear? No. OK thiskey='file'. Good.\n\nHmm, one more subtle thing: in _parse_params, the valid_keys loop checks ALL keys and the LAST match wins (thiskey overwritten each match). For 'file=ansible.ini', only 'file=' matches. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 writing the final code. Also need `from unittest import mock`.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should the helper be a fixture or plain function? Plain function taking monkeypatch+tmp_path fixtures as args \u2014 fine (pytest allows fixtures as args to regular test functions; the helper is called from tests which have monkeypatch/tmp_path). \n\nLet me write it:\n\n```python\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.errors import AnsibleLookupError, AnsibleOptionsError\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup.ini import LookupModule\n\n\nDEFAULT_PARAMS = {\n    'file': 'ansible.ini',\n    'section': 'global',\n    'default': None,\n    're': False,\n    'type': 'ini',\n    'encoding': 'utf-8',\n    'case_sensitive': False,\n    'allow_no_value': False,\n}\n\nBASIC_INI = (\n    '[global]\\n'\n    'key = value\\n'\n    'other = ovalue\\n'\n    '\\n'\n    '[other_section]\\n'\n    'key = ovalue2\\n'\n)\n\n\ndef _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content=BASIC_INI, params=None, **overrides):\n    \"\"\"Build a LookupModule whose option handling, file resolution and data\n    loading are stubbed, so that only LookupModule.run() logic is exercised.\"\"\"\n    if params is None:\n        params = dict(DEFAULT_PARAMS)\n        params.update(overrides)\n\n    ini_path = tmp_path / params['file']\n    ini_path.write_bytes(content.encode(params['encoding']))\n\n    lookup = LookupModule()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', lambda *a, **kw: None)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'get_options', lambda: params)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, '_deprecate_inline_kv', lambda: None)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'find_file_in_search_path', lambda *a, **kw: str(ini_path))\n\n    loader = mock.MagicMock()\n    loader._get_file_contents.return_value = (content.encode(params['encoding']), False)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, '_loader', loader)\n    return lookup\n```\n\nThen the tests. Let me finalize each:\n\n```python\ndef test_simple_key_found(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_multiple_terms_are_accumulated(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key', 'other'], variables={}) == ['value', 'ovalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_insensitive_is_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_finds_exact_case(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n', case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['Key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_miss_falls_back_to_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n', case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_key_without_default_returns_nothing(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_key_with_scalar_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, default='fallback')\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == ['fallback']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_sequence_default_is_extended_item_by_item(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    # get_value() hands back the default untouched; a MutableSequence default\n    # must be appended element by element (lines 199-200)\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, default=['d1', 'd2'])\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == ['d1', 'd2']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_regexp_returns_all_matching_values(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey1 = v1\\nkey2 = v2\\nother = ov\\n', re=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['key.'], variables={}) == ['v1', 'v2']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_parameters(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key file=ansible.ini section=global'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_space_separated_key(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nother key = ovalue\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['other key'], variables={}) == ['ovalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_unknown_option_name(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match='bogus is not a valid option'):\n        lookup.run(['bogus=1'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_value_with_embedded_equals(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\"):\n        lookup.run(['key=a=b'], variables={})\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 match=\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\" \u2014 the message: \"Could not use 'key=a=b' from '['key=a=b']': ...\". re.search(\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\") \u2014 quotes fine. OK.\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_without_any_key(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleOptionsError, match='No key to lookup was provided'):\n        lookup.run(['file=ansible.ini'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_java_properties_type_gets_synthetic_section(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='key=javavalue\\n', type='properties')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['javavalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_duplicate_option_is_reported(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey = a\\nkey = b\\n')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match='Duplicate option'):\n        lookup.run(['key'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_section_is_reported(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, section='nope')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"No section 'nope' in ansible.ini\"):\n        lookup.run(['key'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_allow_no_value_parses_valueless_entries(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nemptykey\\n', allow_no_value=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['emptykey'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_allow_none_is_used_when_allow_no_value_is_absent(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    params = dict(DEFAULT_PARAMS, allow_none=True)\n    del params['allow_no_value']\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nemptykey\\n', params=params)\n    assert lookup.run(['emptykey'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_encoding_option_is_honoured(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey = caf\u00e9\\n', encoding='latin-1')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['caf\u00e9']\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 'caf\u00e9' in source: fine (Python 3 source is UTF-8). The assertion compares 'caf\u00e9' \u2014 to_text(b'caf\\xe9', encoding='latin-1') \u2192 'caf\u00e9'. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 one issue: `ini_path.write_bytes(content.encode('latin-1'))` \u2014 fine.\n\nNow \u2014 double-check `test_inline_space_separated_key` once more with the real _parse_params:\n\nterm 'other key'. paramvals keys (DEFAULT_PARAMS): file, section, default, re, type, encoding, case_sensitive, allow_no_value.\n\n- idp=0, phrase='other': '=' not in \u2192 thiskey='key' (initial). idp==0 \u2192 params['key']='other', keys=['key'].\n- idp=1, phrase='key': '=' not in \u2192 thiskey='key'. params['key']='other' truthy \u2192 params['key'] = 'other key'. keys=['key'].\n\nReturn ['other key']. run: '=' in term? No. ' ' in term.strip()? Yes \u2192 inline path. params=['other key']. Loop: param='other key' \u2192 '=' not in param \u2192 elif key == term: key='other key' == term='other key' \u2192 True \u2192 key='other key', updated_key=True. Then file lookup, parse '[global]\\nother key = ovalue\\n' \u2192 cp: global \u2192 {'other key': 'ovalue'}. get_value('other key','global',None,False) \u2192 cp.get \u2192 optionxform('other key')='other key' \u2192 'ovalue'. ret ['ovalue']. \n\nNow \u2014 hmm, wait: configparser parsing 'other key = ovalue': Let me double-check the option regex more carefully. CPython configparser:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'(?P<vi>[ \\t]*[:=][ \\t]*)'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n)\n```\n\nHmm, roughly (with NOSECT... whatever). For line 'other key = ovalue': option non-greedy up to first [ \\t]*[:=] \u2192 option='other key' (the `\\s*` before '=' consumed by vi group's `[ \\t]*`). Then value='ovalue'. Option name 'other key' \u2014 but wait, is there validation that option names don't contain spaces? In `ConfigParser._read`, after matching OPTCRE, it does `optname = self.optionxform(optname.rstrip())` \u2014 no charset validation (unlike SECTCRE). Actually hmm, older configparser had OPTCRE with `[-\\w\\d.() \"']+`? Let me recall Python 3 configparser:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'\\s*(?P<vi>[=:])\\s*'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n)\n```\n\nAnd in _read: `optname, vi, optval = mo.group('option', 'vi', 'value')`; `optname = self.optionxform(optname.rstrip())`. No restriction on spaces. So 'other key' is fine. I'm fairly confident configparser accepts keys with spaces (e.g., 'foo bar = baz' parses). Yes.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there's one more subtlety: `_read` checks `if not optname` ... and for allow_no_value=False, lines without delimiter raise. Our line has '='. OK.\n\nActually hold on, let me reconsider \u2014 Python 3