file' \u2192 params['file']='file=ansible.ini', keys=['key','file'].\nReturn ['other key','file=ansible.ini'].\nrun: param 'other key' \u2192 no '=' \u2192 key==term ('other key file=ansible.ini' == 'other key file=ansible.ini') \u2192 key='other key', updated_key=True. param 'file=ansible.ini' \u2192 paramvals['file']='ansible.ini'. get_value('other key','global',None,False) \u2192 cp.get('global','other key') \u2192 optionxform \u2192 'other key' \u2192 'ovalue'. ret ['ovalue']. \u2713.\n\nGreat.\n\nNow \u2014 one more: configparser parsing 'other key = ovalue' \u2014 I'm now second-guessing whether configparser allows spaces in option names. Let me verify mentally with CPython source (Lib/configparser.py):\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'(?P<vi>[ \\t]*[:=][ \\t]*)'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n    )\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think it's:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'\\s*(?P<vi>[=:])\\s*'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n    )\n```\n\nEither way, option is `.*?` \u2014 any chars. In `_read`:\n\n```python\n            elif mo:\n                # a non-sect line: option/value pair\n                optname, vi, optval = mo.group('option', 'vi', 'value')\n                optname = self.optionxform(optname.rstrip())\n                if not optname:\n                    e = self._handle_error(...)  # ParsingError\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 `optname.rstrip()` then optionxform. 'other key'.rstrip() \u2192 'other key'. Not empty. Stored. So yes, spaces allowed. (I'm quite sure \u2014 configparser allows arbitrary option names except empty; e.g., 'foo bar = 1' works.)\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually there IS a subtlety: with `.*?` non-greedy and `\\s*(?P<vi>[=:])` \u2014 for 'other key = ovalue', the regex engine finds the FIRST '=' \u2014 option='other key ' \u2192 `\\s*` before '=' is part of vi group... The non-greedy `.*?` tries shortest: 'other key' then `\\s*` matches ' ' then '='. optname='other key' (rstrip no-op). \u2713.\