          "reasoning": "Now I have the actual source. Key findings:\n\n1. `get_value(self, key, section, dflt, is_regexp)`:\n```python\ndef get_value(self, key, section, dflt, is_regexp):\n    if is_regexp:\n        return [v for k, v in self.cp.items(section) if re.match(key, k)]\n    value = None\n    try:\n        value = self.cp.get(section, key)\n    except configparser.NoOptionError:\n        return dflt\n    return value\n```\n\nSo get_value does NOT catch NoSectionError \u2014 `self.cp.items(section)` or `self.cp.get(section, key)` will raise NoSectionError which propagates to run()'s except \u2192 line 196-197 reachable. \n\nAlso important: with `is_regexp=True`, get_value returns a LIST of all matching values \u2192 MutableSequence \u2192 lines 199-200 covered via regexp with multiple matches! And with regexp and no matches \u2192 returns [] (empty list, which is a MutableSequence but falsy... wait, `if var is not None:` \u2014 empty list is not None \u2192 True \u2192 `isinstance([], MutableSequence)` \u2192 True \u2192 loop over empty \u2192 nothing appended. So ret stays [].\n\nSo for the MutableSequence branch, I can use either:\n- re=True with matching keys \u2192 returns list of values \u2192 appended individually.\n- default as list when key missing \u2192 returns dflt (list).\n\nBoth work. The regexp path is more natural. Let me use regexp with multiple matches: file `[global]\\nkey1 = v1\\nkey2 = v2\\n`, term 'key.' with re=True \u2192 get_value returns ['v1', 'v2'] \u2192 ret ['v1', 'v2']. \n\nAlso note: `self.cp.get(section, key)` \u2014 with case_sensitive=False, optionxform lowercases keys in the parser, and `cp.get(section, key)` \u2014 does get() apply optionxform to the key? Yes! `ConfigParser.get(section, option)` calls `optionxform(option)` internally (via `_unify_values`? let me think). Actually, `ConfigParser.get(section, option)` \u2192 `self._unify_values(section, vars)` and `option = self.optionxform(option)`. Yes, in CPython's configparser:\n\n```python\ndef get(self, section, option, *, raw=False, vars=None, fallback=_UNSET):\n    try:\n        d = self._unify_values(section, vars)\n    except NoSectionError:\n        ...\n    option = self.optionxform(option)\n    try:\n        value = d[option]\n    except KeyError:\n        ...\n```\n\nYes, optionxform is applied to the option in get(). So case-insensitive lookup works: 'Key' \u2192 optionxform \u2192 'key' \u2192 matches lowercased stored key. Good.\n\nWith case_sensitive=True (optionxform=to_native), stored keys keep case, and get() looks up exact case. Good.\n\n2. `_parse_params(term, paramvals)`:\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    \"\"\"Safely split parameter term to preserve spaces\"\"\"\n    valid_keys = paramvals.keys()\n    params = defaultdict(lambda: '')\n    keys = []\n    thiskey = 'key'\n    for idp, phrase in enumerate(term.split()):\n        if '=' in phrase:\n            for k in valid_keys:\n                if '%s=' % k in phrase:\n                    thiskey = k\n        if idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]:\n            params[thiskey] = phrase\n            keys.append(thiskey)\n        else:\n            params[thiskey] += ' ' + phrase\n    return [params[x] for x in keys]\n```\n\nInteresting! So `_parse_params` splits on whitespace, and groups phrases by the current \"thiskey\". Let me trace through examples:\n\nExample A: term = 'key file=ansible.ini section=global', paramvals keys = {file, section, default, re, type, encoding, case_sensitive, allow_no_value}.\n\n- idp=0, phrase='key': '=' not in 'key'. thiskey='key'. idp==0 \u2192 params['key']='key', keys=['key'].\n- idp=1, phrase='file=ansible.ini': '=' in phrase \u2192 for k in valid_keys: 'file=' in 'file=ansible.ini' \u2192 yes \u2192 thiskey='file'. (check others: 'section=' in phrase? no. etc.) So thiskey='file'. `idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]` \u2192 params['file'] is '' (defaultdict) \u2192 not params['file'] \u2192 True \u2192 params['file']='file=ansible.ini', keys=['key','file'].\n- idp=2, phrase='section=global': '=' \u2192 'section=' in phrase \u2192 thiskey='section'. params['section']=='' \u2192 params['section']='section=global', keys=['key','file','section'].\n\nReturn ['key', 'file=ansible.ini', 'section=global']. \n\nThen in run(): param 'key' \u2192 no '=' \u2192 key==term \u2192 key='key', updated_key=True. param 'file=ansible.ini' \u2192 split('=') \u2192 ['file','ansible.ini'] \u2192 'file' in paramvals \u2192 paramvals['file']='ansible.ini'. param 'section=global' \u2192 paramvals['section']='global'. \n\nExample B: term = 'key=a=b':\n- idp=0, phrase='key=a=b': '=' in phrase \u2192 for k in valid_keys: 'key=' in 'key=a=b'? Is 'key' a valid key? paramvals keys: file, section, default, re, type, encoding, case_sensitive, allow_no_value. 'key=' \u2014 hmm, does any valid key k satisfy '%s=' % k in 'key=a=b'? 'key' is not in paramvals (the option is named... wait, is there a 'key' option? No). Check each: 'file=' in 'key=a=b'? No. 'section='? No. 'default='? No. 're='? No. 'type='? No. 'encoding='? No. 'case_sensitive='? No. 'allow_no_value='? No. So thiskey stays 'key'.\n- idp==0 \u2192 params['key'] = 'key=a=b', keys=['key'].\nReturn ['key=a=b'].\n\nThen run(): param='key=a=b' \u2192 '=' in param \u2192 split('=') \u2192 ['key','a','b'] \u2192 ValueError \u2192 line 167. \n\nExample C: term = 'key bogus=1':\n- idp=0, 'key': no '=' \u2192 params['key']='key', keys=['key'].\n- idp=1, 'bogus=1': '=' in phrase \u2192 check valid keys: 'bogus=' not among them... wait, check: does any k in valid_keys have '%s=' % k in 'bogus=1'? 'file=' no, 'section=' no, 'default=' no, 're=' no, 'type=' no, 'encoding=' no, 'case_sensitive=' no, 'allow_no_value=' no. So thiskey stays 'key' (from previous iteration? thiskey persists across loop iterations \u2014 it was 'key' initially and unchanged). So thiskey='key'. `idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]` \u2192 params['key'] = 'key' (truthy) \u2192 else branch \u2