se case appears unreachable (I shouldn't worry about it; coverage tools would mark the branch partially covered, but that's inherent to the code).\n\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 one more scenario: term with '=' phrase matching a valid key at idp=0, e.g. 'file=x.ini': params=['file=x.ini'] \u2192 if-branch \u2192 updated_key False \u2192 error. Covered by my no-key test.\n\nAnd what about a term like 'key=value' where 'key' is not a valid option \u2192 error at line 159. Covered by 'key bogus=1' \u2192 actually that gives name='key bogus'. Let me also do a cleaner one: term 'bogus=1' \u2192 idp=0, '=' in phrase \u2192 check valid keys \u2014 none match 'bogus=' \u2192 thiskey='key' \u2192 params['key']='bogus=1' \u2192 return ['bogus=1'] \u2192 run: split \u2192 name='bogus' not in paramvals \u2192 AnsibleLookupError('bogus is not a valid option.'). Cleaner. I'll use 'bogus=1'.\n\nHmm wait, but careful \u2014 're=' check: is 're=' in 'bogus=1'? No. OK. But watch out for substring traps: e.g., phrase 'type=text' \u2014 'type=' matches k='type'. Also 're=' would match inside 'type='? '%s=' % 're' = 're=' \u2014 is 're=' in 'type=text'? 'type=text' contains 'e=t'... 're=' \u2014 letters 'r','e','=' \u2014 'type=text' \u2192 t,y,p,e,=,... no 're=' substring. OK. But e.g. 'store=x' would contain 'ore='... not 're='. 'more=x' contains 're='! ('m-o-r-e-=' \u2192 're=' at index 2). So a phrase 'more=x' would set thiskey='re'. Fun, but not needed.\n\nNow, the space-in-term branch: `' ' in term.strip()` without '='. E.g., term 'key with spaces'? Hmm \u2014 but wait, can an ini key have spaces? With configparser, 'key with spaces' as an option name \u2014 allowed? Option names can contain spaces if written without delimiters... 'key with spaces = value' \u2014 configparser would parse option 'key with spaces'? Actually the option name regex `[0-9A-Za-z_...]*` \u2014 hmm, configparser's OPTCRE allows almost anything before the delimiter except leading whitespace and containing del