onfigparser:\n\n```python\ndef get(self, section, option, *, raw=False, vars=None, fallback=_UNSET):\n    try:\n        d = self._unify_values(section, vars)\n    except NoSectionError:\n        ...\n    option = self.optionxform(option)\n    try:\n        value = d[option]\n    except KeyError:\n        ...\n```\n\nYes, optionxform is applied to the option in get(). So case-insensitive lookup works: 'Key' \u2192 optionxform \u2192 'key' \u2192 matches lowercased stored key. Good.\n\nWith case_sensitive=True (optionxform=to_native), stored keys keep case, and get() looks up exact case. Good.\n\n2. `_parse_params(term, paramvals)`:\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    \"\"\"Safely split parameter term to preserve spaces\"\"\"\n    valid_keys = paramvals.keys()\n    params = defaultdict(lambda: '')\n    keys = []\n    thiskey = 'key'\n    for idp, phrase in enumerate(term.split()):\n        if '=' in phrase:\n            for k in valid_keys:\n                if '%s=' % k in phrase:\n                    thiskey = k\n        if idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]:\n            params[thiskey] = phrase\n            keys.append(thiskey)\n        else:\n            params[thiskey] += ' ' + phrase\n    return [params[x] for x in keys]\n```\n\nInteresting! So `_parse_params` splits on whitespace, and groups phrases by the current \"thiskey\". Let me trace through examples:\n\nExample A: term = 'key file=ansible.ini section=global', paramvals keys = {file, section, default, re, type, encoding, case_sensitive, allow_no_value}.\n\n- idp=0, phrase='key': '=' not in 'key'. thiskey='key'. idp==0 \u2192 params['key']='key', keys=['key'].\n- idp=1, phrase='file=ansible.ini': '=' in phrase \u2192 for k in valid_keys: 'file=' in 'file=ansible.ini' \u2192 yes \u2192 thiskey='file'. (check others: 'section=' in phrase? no. etc.) So thiskey='file'. `idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]` \u2192 params['file'] is '' (defaultdict) \u2192 not params['file'] \u2192 True \u2192 params['