ey, section, dflt, is_regexp):\n    if is_regexp:\n        return [v for k, v in self.cp.items(section) if re.match(key, k)]\n    value = None\n    try:\n        value = self.cp.get(section, key)\n    except configparser.NoOptionError:\n        return dflt\n    return value\n```\n\nThis matches ansible-core 2.12+ (after the NoSectionError refactor). And run() matches the snippet. Good.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 one thing: in run(), `var = self.get_value(key, paramvals['section'], paramvals['default'], paramvals['re'])`. get_value's regexp branch returns a list \u2014 so `isinstance(var, MutableSequence)` \u2192 True \u2192 extend. And non-regexp returns str or dflt. Consistent with my tests.\n\nNow \u2014 the `default` option: in real DOCUMENTATION, default is a string. My list-default test is a bit artificial but valid for coverage. Keep it, with a comment.\n\nLet me also double check `_parse_params` edge: term 'key bogus=1' \u2014 I decided to use 'bogus=1' instead for a cleaner name. Trace 'bogus=1': idp=0, '=' in phrase \u2192 loop valid_keys: does any '%s=' % k appear in 'bogus=1'? 'file=' no, 'section=' no, 'default=' no, 're=' no, 'type=' no, 'encoding=' no, 'case_sensitive=' no, 'allow_no_value=' no. thiskey='key'. params['key']='bogus=1'. Return ['bogus=1']. run: split('=') \u2192 ['bogus','1'] \u2192 'bogus' not in paramvals \u2192 AnsibleLookupError('bogus is not a valid option.'). \n\nHmm wait \u2014 careful: 're=' \u2014 is 're=' a substring of 'bogus=1'? b-o-g-u-s-=-1 \u2192 no 're='. Good.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 what about 'type=' vs phrase 'subtype=x'? Not used. OK.\n\nNow, also \u2014 the inline test 'key file=ansible.ini section=global': note 'file=' check happens for k='file' \u2014 but ALSO check: does 're=' appear in 'file=ansible.ini'? No. Does 'type=' appear? No. OK thiskey='file'. Good.\n\nHmm, one more subtle thing: in _parse_params, the valid_keys loop checks ALL keys and the LAST match wins (thiskey overwritten each match). For 'file=ansible.ini', only 'file=' matches. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 writing the final code. Also need `from unittest import mock`.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should the helper be a fixture or plain function? Plain function taking monkeypatch+tmp_path fixtures as args \u2014 fine (pytest allows fixtures as args to regular test functions; the helper is called from tests which have monkeypatch/tmp_path). \n\nLet me write it:\n\n```python\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.errors import AnsibleLookupError, AnsibleOptionsError\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup.ini import LookupModule\n\n\nDEFAULT_PARAMS = {\n    'file': 'ansible.ini',\n    'section': 'global',\n    'default': None,\n    're': False,\n    'type': 'ini',\n    'encoding': 'utf-8',\n    'case_sensitive': False,\n    'allow_no_value': False,\n}\n\nBASIC_INI = (\n    '[global]\\n'\n    'key = value\\n'\n    'other = ovalue\\n'\n    '\\n'\n    '[other_section]\\n'\n    'key = ovalue2\\n'\n)\n\n\ndef _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content=BASIC_INI, params=None, **overrides):\n    \"\"\"Build a LookupModule whose option handling, file resolution and data\n    loading are stubbed, so that only LookupModule.run() logic is exercised.\"\"\"\n    if params is None:\n        params = dict(DEFAULT_PARAMS)\n        params.update(overrides)\n\n    ini_path = tmp_path / params['file']\n    ini_path.write_bytes(content.encode(params['encoding']))\n\n    lookup = LookupModule()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', lambda *a, **kw: None)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'get_options', lambda: params)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, '_deprecate_inline_kv', lambda: None)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'find_file_in_search_path', lambda *a, **kw: str(ini_path))\n\n    loader = mock.MagicMock()\n    loader._get_file_contents.return_value = (content.encode(params['encoding']), False)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, '_loader', loader)\n    return lookup\n```\n\nThen the tests. Let me finalize each:\n\n```python\ndef test_simple_key_found(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_multiple_terms_are_accumulated(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key', 'other'], variables={}) == ['value', 'ovalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_insensitive_is_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_finds_exact_case(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n', case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['Key'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_miss_falls_back_to_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nKey = value\\n', case_sensitive=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_key_without_default_returns_nothing(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_key_with_scalar_default(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, default='fallback')\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == ['fallback']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_sequence_default_is_extended_item_by_item(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    # get_value() hands back the default untouched; a MutableSequence default\n    # must be appended element by element (lines 199-200)\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, default=['d1', 'd2'])\n    assert lookup.run(['missing'], variables={}) == ['d1', 'd2']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_regexp_returns_all_matching_values(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey1 = v1\\nkey2 = v2\\nother = ov\\n', re=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['key.'], variables={}) == ['v1', 'v2']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_parameters(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    assert lookup.run(['key file=ansible.ini section=global'], variables={}) == ['value']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_space_separated_key(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nother key = ovalue\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['other key'], variables={}) == ['ovalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_unknown_option_name(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match='bogus is not a valid option'):\n        lookup.run(['bogus=1'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_value_with_embedded_equals(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\"):\n        lookup.run(['key=a=b'], variables={})\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 match=\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\" \u2014 the message: \"Could not use 'key=a=b' from '['key=a=b']': ...\". re.search(\"Could not use 'key=a=b'\") \u2014 quotes fine. OK.\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_without_any_key(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path)\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleOptionsError, match='No key to lookup was provided'):\n        lookup.run(['file=ansible.ini'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_java_properties_type_gets_synthetic_section(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='key=javavalue\\n', type='properties')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['javavalue']\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_duplicate_option_is_reported(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey = a\\nkey = b\\n')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match='Duplicate option'):\n        lookup.run(['key'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_section_is_reported(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, section='nope')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"No section 'nope' in ansible.ini\"):\n        lookup.run(['key'], variables={})\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_allow_no_value_parses_valueless_entries(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nemptykey\\n', allow_no_value=True)\n    assert lookup.run(['emptykey'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_allow_none_is_used_when_allow_no_value_is_absent(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    params = dict(DEFAULT_PARAMS, allow_none=True)\n    del params['allow_no_value']\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nemptykey\\n', params=params)\n    assert lookup.run(['emptykey'], variables={}) == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_encoding_option_is_honoured(monkeypatch, tmp_path):\n    lookup = _make_lookup(monkeypatch, tmp_path, content='[global]\\nkey = caf\u00e9\\n', encoding='latin-1')\n    assert lookup.run(['key'], variables={}) == ['caf\u00e9']\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 'caf\u00e9' in source: fine (Python 3 source is UTF-8). The assertion compares 'caf\u00e9' \u2014 to_text(b'caf\\xe9', encoding='latin-1') \u2192 'caf\u00e9'. Good.\n\nWait \u2014 one issue: `ini_path.write_bytes(content.encode('latin-1'))` \u2014 fine.\n\nNow \u2014 double-check `test_inline_space_separated_key` once more with the real _parse_params:\n\nterm 'other key'. paramvals keys (DEFAULT_PARAMS): file, section, default, re, type, encoding, case_sensitive, allow_no_value.\n\n- idp=0, phrase='other': '=' not in \u2192 thiskey='key' (initial). idp==0 \u2192 params['key']='other', keys=['key'].\n- idp=1, phrase='key': '=' not in \u2192 thiskey='key'. params['key']='other' truthy \u2192 params['key'] = 'other key'. keys=['key'].\n\nReturn ['other key']. run: '=' in term? No. ' ' in term.strip()? Yes \u2192 inline path. params=['other key']. Loop: param='other key' \u2192 '=' not in param \u2192 elif key == term: key='other key' == term='other key' \u2192 True \u2192 key='other key', updated_key=True. Then file lookup, parse '[global]\\nother key = ovalue\\n' \u2192 cp: global \u2192 {'other key': 'ovalue'}. get_value('other key','global',None,False) \u2192 cp.get \u2192 optionxform('other key')='other key' \u2192 'ovalue'. ret ['ovalue']. \n\nNow \u2014 hmm, wait: configparser parsing 'other key = ovalue': Let me double-check the option regex more carefully. CPython configparser:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'(?P<vi>[ \\t]*[:=][ \\t]*)'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n)\n```\n\nHmm, roughly (with NOSECT... whatever). For line 'other key = ovalue': option non-greedy up to first [ \\t]*[:=] \u2192 option='other key' (the `\\s*` before '=' consumed by vi group's `[ \\t]*`). Then value='ovalue'. Option name 'other key' \u2014 but wait, is there validation that option names don't contain spaces? In `ConfigParser._read`, after matching OPTCRE, it does `optname = self.optionxform(optname.rstrip())` \u2014 no charset validation (unlike SECTCRE). Actually hmm, older configparser had OPTCRE with `[-\\w\\d.() \"']+`? Let me recall Python 3 configparser:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'\\s*(?P<vi>[=:])\\s*'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n)\n```\n\nAnd in _read: `optname, vi, optval = mo.group('option', 'vi', 'value')`; `optname = self.optionxform(optname.rstrip())`. No restriction on spaces. So 'other key' is fine. I'm fairly confident configparser accepts keys with spaces (e.g., 'foo bar = baz' parses). Yes.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there's one more subtlety: `_read` checks `if not optname` ... and for allow_no_value=False, lines without delimiter raise. Our line has '='. OK.\n\nActually hold on, let me reconsider \u2014 Python 3's configparser OPTCRE:\n\n```python\nOPTCRE = re.compile(\n    r'(?P<option>.*?)'\n    r'(?P<vi>[ \\t]*[:=][ \\t]*)'\n    r'(?P<value>.*)$'\n    )\n```\n\nHmm, and there's also a check: `if mo.group('option').strip() == ''`... something. 'other key' is fine.\n\nOK. But you know, to reduce risk, maybe I should avoid the space-in-key trick and instead assert the simpler behavior: term 'other key' with the BASIC_INI (which has no 'other key' option) \u2192 key='other key' \u2192 NoOptionError \u2192 default None \u2192 ret []. That covers the same lines with a simpler file. But the assertion [] is weaker (could pass for wrong reasons... well, no \u2014 if the inline path weren't taken, key would be 'other key' anyway (key=term) \u2192 same result! Hmm \u2014 actually if the inline branch were skipped entirely, key = term = 'other key' \u2192 same lookup \u2192 same []. So the test wouldn't distinguish. With the space-key file, the test DOES distinguish: if inline parsing broke, key would still be 'other key'... hmm wait, same thing. The inline path with ONE param that equals the whole term \u2014 key ends up = term either way!\n\nHmm right: with term 'other key', whether or not the inline branch runs, key = 'other key'. So the test verifies nothing about the inline path except that it doesn't crash. To make it meaningful, the file should have BOTH 'other key' and something that distinguishes... Actually the distinguishing factor: if the inline path ran and _parse_params returned ['other', 'key'] (hypothetically), key would be 'other' \u2192 different result. With the real _parse_params merging, key='other key'. So the test verifies _parse_params' space-preserving merge! That's actually meaningful: it tests that 'other key' is treated as ONE key with the space preserved (the \"Safely split parameter term to preserve spaces\" docstring). If the merge broke (e.g., key became 'other'), the lookup would fail \u2192 []. So asserting ['ovalue'] verifies the merge behavior. Good, keep it.\n\nAlternatively, use a term with an inline option AND spaces in the key: e.g. term 'other key file=ansible.ini' \u2192 phrases ['other','key','file=ansible.ini'] \u2192 idp=0 'other' \u2192 params['key']='other'; idp=1 'key' \u2192 merged \u2192 'other key'; idp=2 'file=ansible.ini' \u2192 thiskey='file' \u2192 params['file']='file=ansible.ini', keys=['key','file']. Return ['other key', 'file=ansible.ini']. run: param 'other key' \u2192 no '=' \u2192 key==term \u2192 key='other key', updated_key=True. param 'file=...' \u2192 paramvals['file']='ansible.ini'. Lookup 'other key' \u2192 'ovalue'. This covers MORE: the multi-param path with a spaced key. Nice \u2014 this is a better test. Let me use term 'other key file=ansible.ini' with content '[global]\\nother key = ovalue\\n'. Assert ['ovalue'].\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 'file=' check for phrase 'file=ansible.ini': thiskey='file'. But ALSO \u2014 careful \u2014 does 'other key' phrase contain '='? No. Good.\n\nHmm, but one more check on _parse_params for idp=2: `if idp == 0 or not params[thiskey]` \u2192 params['file'] is '' \u2192 True \u2192 assign. keys=['key','file']. Return ['other key', 'file=ansible.ini']. \n\nThen run: updated_key=True. Good. This test covers: space branch, '=' branch of line 150 (both conditions \u2014 well, the or), 151-152, 156 False + 161 True (param 1), 156 True + 158 True + 160 (param 2), 168 True. Excellent \u2014 this single test covers a lot. I'll keep BOTH inline tests (the '='-heavy one and this one).\n\nNow \u2014 let me reconsider `test_inline_parameters` term: 'key file=ansible.ini section=global'. Fine as analyzed.\n\nNow \u2014 one more possible issue: `monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'get_options', lambda: params)` \u2014 but `get_options` might be a method that takes no args \u2014 yes. OK.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, `set_options` real method might be needed to be called with the mock recording? Not needed.\n\nNow \u2014 is there any chance that `LookupModule.run` uses `self._templar` or `self._display`? Not in the snippet. OK.\n\nNow \u2014 imports in the test file: `from unittest import mock`, `import pytest`, `from ansible.errors import ...`, `from ansible.plugins.lookup.ini import LookupModule`. \n\nWait \u2014 should I import `MutableSequence` to double-check the list-default test? Not needed for assertions.\n\nOne more consideration \u2014 the task says \"always making sure that each test is correct and indeed improves coverage\". I believe the tests do. Let me also sanity-check the `test_inline_value_with_embedded_equals` once more with real _parse_params:\n\nterm 'key=a=b'. idp=0, phrase='key=a=b': '=' in phrase \u2192 loop valid_keys: any '%s=' % k in 'key=a=b'? Check 'file=' no; 'section=' no; 'default=' no; 're=' no; 'type=' no; 'encoding=' no; 'case_sensitive=' no; 'allow_no_value=' no. \u2192 thiskey stays 'key'. idp==0 \u2192 params['key']='key=a=b'. Return ['key=a=b'].\n\nrun: '=' in term \u2192 inline. params=['key=a=b']. param='key=a=b' \u2192 '=' in param \u2192 name, value = param.split('=') \u2192 ValueError (3 values) \u2192 except ValueError \u2192 AnsibleLookupError(\"Could not use 'key=a=b' from '['key=a=b']': too many values to unpack (expected 2)\"). Match \"Could not use 'key=a=b'\" \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, `to_native(e)` where e is ValueError \u2192 str(e) = 'too many values to unpack (expected 2)'. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_inline_unknown_option_name` with term 'bogus=1': as traced \u2192 AnsibleLookupError('bogus is not a valid option.'). \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 but wait, one more check on _parse_params for 'bogus=1': the valid_keys loop \u2014 `if '%s=' % k in phrase` \u2014 for k='re', 're=' in 'bogus=1'? No. OK. But hmm \u2014 what about k='type'... no. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_inline_without_any_key` term 'file=ansible.ini': traced \u2192 params=['file=ansible.ini'] \u2192 paramvals['file']='ansible.ini' \u2192 updated_key False \u2192 AnsibleOptionsError. \u2713. Note: the error is raised BEFORE file access \u2192 loader mock unused. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_java_properties_type_gets_synthetic_section`: content 'key=javavalue\\n', type='properties'. run: term 'key' \u2192 no inline. path \u2192 ini_path. config=StringIO. type=='properties' \u2192 write '[java_properties]\\n', paramvals['section']='java_properties'. contents \u2192 to_text \u2192 write. readfp \u2192 section java_properties, option key \u2192 'javavalue'. get_value('key','java_properties',None,False) \u2192 'javavalue'. ret ['javavalue']. \u2713.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I assert `paramvals['section']` was mutated? The params dict is accessible via the closure... I could capture it. Eh \u2014 the result assertion suffices (if section weren't 'java_properties', cp.get('global',...) \u2192 NoSectionError \u2192 test fails). Good implicit assertion.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_duplicate_option_is_reported`: content '[global]\\nkey = a\\nkey = b\\n'. readfp \u2192 DuplicateOptionError. \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 one concern: configparser strict parsing \u2014 'key = a' then 'key = b' in same section \u2192 DuplicateOptionError with strict=True (default). \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_missing_section_is_reported`: section='nope'. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_allow_no_value_parses_valueless_entries`: content '[global]\\nemptykey\\n', allow_no_value=True. readfp OK. get_value('emptykey','global',None,False) \u2192 cp.get \u2192 None (value is None). var=None \u2192 skip \u2192 ret []. \u2713. \n\nHmm wait \u2014 cp.get returns None for a no-value option? `d[option]` \u2192 None. Yes. And `if var is not None` \u2192 False \u2192 ret []. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_allow_none_is_used_when_allow_no_value_is_absent`: same but via allow_none fallback. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_encoding_option_is_honoured`: \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_regexp_returns_all_matching_values`: content '[global]\\nkey1 = v1\\nkey2 = v2\\nother = ov\\n', re=True, term 'key.'. get_value \u2192 cp.items('global') \u2192 [('key1','v1'),('key2','v2'),('other','ov')] \u2192 re.match('key.','key1') \u2713, 'key2' \u2713, 'other' \u2717 \u2192 ['v1','v2'] \u2192 MutableSequence \u2192 ret ['v1','v2']. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 `test_sequence_default_is_extended_item_by_item`: default=['d1','d2'], term 'missing' \u2192 NoOptionError \u2192 dflt \u2192 ['d1','d2'] \u2192 ret ['d1','d2']. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing I want to double check: `cp.get(section, key)` when the option exists with value None (allow_no_value) \u2014 returns None, NOT raising NoOptionError. Right \u2014 the option exists in the section dict. \u2713.\n\nAnd when option missing \u2192 NoOptionError \u2192 dflt. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 about `variables={}` vs None: run(terms, variables={}) \u2192 set_options(var_options={}) mocked; find_file_in_search_path({}, 'files', ...) mocked. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 potential problem: `monkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', lambda *a, **kw: None)` \u2014 monkeypatch requires the attribute to exist unless raising=False. `set_options` exists on AnsiblePlugin class \u2192 hasattr True. `get_options` exists. `_deprecate_inline_kv` exists (per get_info). `find_file_in_search_path` exists. `_loader` exists (instance attr None). All good.\n\nNow \u2014 one more thought: should I also verify that `set_options` was CALLED with the right args (line 138 postcondition)? Could capture via a recorder:\n\n```python\ncalls = {}\ndef fake_set_options(*a, **kw):\n    calls.update(kw)\nmonkeypatch.setattr(lookup, 'set_options', fake_set_options)\n...\nassert calls == {'var_options': {}, 'direct': {}}\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 that's nice for verifying line 138's postcondition. But it complicates the helper. Maybe add an optional `record` dict param... Let me keep it simple: in ONE test (test_simple_key_found), I could verify set_options/get_options interaction. Actually, let me add a small verification in the helper via a returned handle? Overkill. \n\nAlternative: make the helper return (lookup, params) and in one test assert params mutation for inline tests:\n\nFor `test_inline_parameters`, after run, `params['file']` should be 'ansible.ini' (unchanged) and `params['section']`='global' (set from inline). Actually paramvals['section'] was already 'global'. Let me use a different section in inline: term 'key file=ansible.ini section=other_section' \u2192 paramvals['section'] becomes 'other_section' \u2192 get_value looks in other_section \u2192 'ovalue2'. That's a strong postcondition: inline params actually override options! \n\nLet me redo: BASIC_INI has [other_section] with key=ovalue2. term 'key section=other_section' \u2192 params: idp=0 'key' \u2192 params['key']='key'; idp=1 'section=other_section' \u2192 thiskey='section' \u2192 params['section']='section=other_section'. Return ['key','section=other_section']. run: key='key'; paramvals['section']='other_section'. get_value('key','other_section',...) \u2192 'ovalue2'. ret ['ovalue2']. \n\nSo `test_inline_parameters`: term 'key section=other_section' \u2192 ['ovalue2']. This proves inline override works. And also include file override? term 'key file=ansible.ini section=other_section' \u2192 both. Let me use that \u2014 covers two inline options. Result ['ovalue2']. \n\nAnd I can additionally assert the params dict was mutated: after run, `params['section'] == 'other_section'`. To do that, the helper must expose params. Let me have the helper return a tuple? That changes all call sites... Alternatively, attach params to the lookup: `lookup._test_params = params`? Hacky. Or just have the helper return lookup and separately build params in the test... \n\nSimplest: helper returns lookup; for the mutation assertion, I can check `lookup.cp` sections? No. Hmm \u2014 actually I can verify the override effect purely via the result ('ovalue2' comes only from other_section). That's sufficient postcondition. Skip dict introspection.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there's one more subtlety with inline overriding 'file': term 'key file=ansible.ini section=other_sec