w \u2014 let me also think: do I want a test for `variables=None`? Not needed.\n\nNow \u2014 one more consideration on test independence and state pollution: `LookupModule()` per test; monkeypatch undoes instance attrs; tmp_path unique per test. The `params` dict per test. No module-level mutation. \u2713.\n\nAlso \u2014 deprecation warnings: `_deprecate_inline_kv` mocked \u2192 no warnings. `readfp` DeprecationWarning \u2014 pytest may show warnings but not fail (unless filterwarnings=error in setup.cfg \u2014 ansible's pytest.ini? ansible's setup.cfg has `filterwarnings = error`?? Hmm \u2014 ansible's test config: test/units/pytest.ini? I don't think ansible turns warnings into errors for unit tests. Actually ansible's setup.cfg has:\n\n```\n[tool:pytest]\n...\nfilterwarnings =\n    error:::ansible.*  ?\n```\n\nHmm, I recall ansible unit tests do run with some warning filters... In ansible's setup.cfg:\n\n```\n[tool:pytest]\ntestpaths = test/units\naddopts = -r x --durations=20\n```\n\nI don't recall filterwarnings=error. But to be safe, should I avoid `readfp`? I can't \u2014 run() calls it. If warnings were errors, the duplicate-option test and all readfp tests would fail... but then the module itself would be untestable, which contradicts the task. So fine.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. Python 3.12+ DeprecationWarning for readfp: \"This method is deprecated, use read_file() instead\". If the test env has -W error... unlikely. Proceed.\n\nNow \u2014 one more thing: the `match` for pytest.raises uses `re.search`. Pattern \"No section 'nope' in ansible.ini\" \u2014 contains no special regex chars except quotes (fine) and dots? 'ansible.ini' \u2014 '.' matches any char \u2014 fine (matches literal '.'). OK.\n\nPattern 'Duplicate option' fine. Pattern 'bogus is not a valid option' fine. Pattern \"Could not use 'key=a=b'\" fine. Pattern 'No key to lookup was provided' fine.\n\nNow \u2014 let me also think about whether `AnsibleLookupError` message for missing section