o patching `configparser.ConfigParser.readfp = configparser.ConfigParser.read_file` (or adding the attribute) would work globally.\n\n   But careful: patching a stdlib class globally \u2014 use monkeypatch.setattr on the class: `monkeypatch.setattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp', configparser.ConfigParser.read_file, raising=False)`. Wait \u2014 but `read_file` signature: `read_file(f, source=None)`. `readfp(f)` called as `self.cp.readfp(config)` \u2192 bound \u2192 read_file(config) \u2192 fine. And `source=None` \u2192 no source in error messages. DuplicateOptionError message would then be \"while parsing from stream: ...\" hmm, with source=None the message is \"while parsing from stream: option 'key' in section 'global' already exists\"? Hmm, actually when source is None, ParsingError.formatmessage returns \"while parsing from stream\" hmm... Let me check: \n\n   ```python\n   def formatmessage(self):\n       if self.source is None:\n           return \"while parsing from stream\"\n       else:\n           return \"while parsing from %s\" % self.source\n   ```\n\n   Hmm, not exactly \u2014 I think it's:\n\n   ```python\n   def formatmessage(self):\n       if self.source is None:\n           return \"while parsing from stream\"\n       return \"while parsing from %s\" % self.source\n   ```\n\n   Hmm, whatever \u2014 the AnsibleLookupError message \"Duplicate option in 'ansible.ini': {error}\" contains \"Duplicate option\" regardless. My match 'Duplicate option' still works.\n\n   But wait \u2014 is patching the stdlib class acceptable? It's a compatibility shim. monkeypatch will undo it after each test. But it needs to be applied in every test that reaches readfp \u2014 i.e., in the helper `_make_lookup`. That's clean: add to the helper:\n\n   ```python\n   if not hasattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp'):\n       monkeypatch.setattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp', configparser.ConfigParser.read_file, raising=False)\n   ```\n\n   Hmm, but monkeypatch.setattr with raising=False on the class \u2014 records and removes on teardown. Since the attribute doesn't exist, teardown deletes it. Good, no pollution.\n\n   Actually, better: always set it (raising=False) so behavior is consistent:\n\n   ```python\n   monkeypatch.setattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp', configparser.ConfigParser.read_file, raising=False)\n   ```\n\n   Wait \u2014 but if readfp DOES exist (older Python), setting it to read_file changes behavior slightly (readfp emits DeprecationWarning; read_file doesn't). Functionally identical. Hmm, but on older Pythons, readfp is literally `read_file = readfp`? Actually in 3.2-3.11: `def readfp(self, fp, source=None): ... deprecated warning ... self.read_file(fp, source)`. So aliasing to read_file is equivalent minus the warning. Fine.\n\n   Actually, to be safe across versions, only patch if missing:\n\n   ```python\n   if not hasattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp'):\n       monkeypatch.setattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp', configparser.ConfigParser.read_file, raising=False)\n   ```\n\n   That's the cleanest compat shim.\n\n   But hold on \u2014 which `configparser` should I import in the test? The plugin uses `ansible.module_utils.six.moves.configparser`. On Python 3, `six.moves.configparser` IS the stdlib `configparser` module (six maps it via `import configparser`... actually six.moves.configparser is a lazy module proxy to the stdlib configparser on py3). So `configparser.ConfigParser` in the test (importing stdlib directly) is the same class object as what the plugin uses. To be extra safe, import the same way the plugin does: `from ansible.module_utils.six.moves import configparser`. That guarantees identity. Let me do that.\n\n2. Alternative: patch `LookupModule.cp`? No \u2014 cp is created inside run().\n\n3. Alternative: mock the ConfigParser entirely? Too invasive.\n\nOption 1 is right. Let me verify: does `read_file` exist on ConfigParser in this Python? Yes \u2014 `read_file(f, source=None)` exists since 3.2. \n\nHmm wait, one more consideration: `readfp(fp)` vs `read_file(fp, source=None)` \u2014 when called as `self.cp.readfp(config)`, with our shim `readfp = read_file` (an unbound function assigned as class attr), `self.cp.readfp(config)` \u2192 `read_file(self.cp, config)` \u2192 works. \u2713.\n\nNow \u2014 also check: are there other Python-version issues? `to_text(contents, errors='surrogate_or_strict', encoding=...)` \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 the error occurred at line 190 in the FIRST test. After the shim, all tests should proceed. But let me double check other potential issues that were masked:\n\n- `test_set_options_receives_variables_and_direct_kwargs` \u2014 after shim, run proceeds: find_file mocked \u2192 path; StringIO; type 'ini'; contents; readfp\u2192read_file shim; get_value('key','global',None,False) \u2192 cp.get \u2192 'value'. ret ['value']. \u2713.\n\n- DuplicateOptionError test: read_file with strict=True \u2192 DuplicateOptionError. \u2713.\n\nWait \u2014 one more thing about the shim and `raising=False`: monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value, raising=False) \u2014 allows setting non-existent attribute. On undo, it deletes the attribute (since it didn't exist). \u2713.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 there's a subtlety: patching the CLASS affects all instances globally during the test \u2014 that's what we want, and monkeypatch undoes it. No pollution. \u2713.\n\nAlso \u2014 should I apply the shim in the helper only, or module-level? Module-level patching would pollute global state across the whole test session (and can't be undone cleanly without a fixture). Helper-level with monkeypatch is per-test and auto-undone. \u2713.\n\nLet me also double check `hasattr(configparser.ConfigParser, 'readfp')` on Python 3.12: removed \u2192 False \u2192 patch applied. On 3.11: True \u2192 no patch \u2192 readfp works (with deprecation warning). \u2713.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, actually let me reconsider: is it possible that the environment's